--- a/docs/1.guide/1.basics/6.error.md
+++ b/docs/1.guide/1.basics/6.error.md
@@ -75,6 +75,46 @@ This will end the request with `400 - Bad Request` status code and the following
 > [!IMPORTANT]
 > Error `statusText` should be short (max 512 to 1024 characters) and only include tab, spaces or visible ASCII characters and extended characters (byte value 128–255). Prefer `message` in JSON body for extended message.
 
+## Problem Details (RFC 9457)
+
+Use `problem()` to respond with [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html) problem details. It returns an `HTTPError`, so it can be thrown or returned like any other error.
+
+```js
+import { problem } from "h3";
+
+app.post("/transfers", (event) => {
+  throw problem({
+    status: 422,
+    statusText: "Unprocessable Content",
+    message: "Insufficient funds",
+    type: "https://example.com/errors/insufficient-funds",
+    instance: "/transfers/7",
+  });
+});
+```
+
+This ends the request with `422` and `Content-Type: application/problem+json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "https://example.com/errors/insufficient-funds",
+  "title": "Unprocessable Content",
+  "status": 422,
+  "detail": "Insufficient funds",
+  "instance": "/transfers/7"
+}
+```
+
+- `type` defaults to `about:blank` when omitted.
+- `title` comes from `statusText`, and `detail` comes from `message`. Both are left out when absent.
+- `instance` is left out when omitted.
+- `body` members are added at the top level. RFC members win on collision.
+- Unhandled `problem()` errors hide `detail` and `body`, as `HTTPError` does.
+- Only errors created by `problem()` use this format. Other `HTTPError` responses keep the JSON format above.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> If [`onError`](/guide/api/h3#global-hooks) returns a different value, that value becomes the response and the problem format is not applied. Clients that only accept `application/json` will not accept `application/problem+json` responses.
+
 ## Unhandled Errors
 
 Any error that occurs during calling [request lifecycle](/guide/basics/lifecycle) without using `HTTPError` will be processed as an _unhandled_ error.
